{"edits": [{"finding_ids": ["app/tickets.py:11:config-class"], "search": "from pydantic import BaseModel", "replace": "from pydantic import BaseModel, ConfigDict", "explanation": "Add the ConfigDict import needed for the V2 `model_config` declaration (migration.md#changes-to-config: config is set via `model_config = ConfigDict(...)`)."}, {"finding_ids": ["app/tickets.py:11:config-class"], "search": "    class Config:
        anystr_strip_whitespace = True", "replace": "    model_config = ConfigDict(str_strip_whitespace=True)", "explanation": "The V1 `class Config` is deprecated in V2 (migration.md#changes-to-config). Replaced with `model_config = ConfigDict(...)`, and the renamed setting `anystr_strip_whitespace` became `str_strip_whitespace`, with the value unchanged."}], "notes": "Two edits, non-overlapping, applied in order. The import is updated first, then the class block is replaced.

Behavior risk not covered by the finding, so left unchanged: `from_db_rows` passes integer primary keys from the driver into `ticket_id: str`. Pydantic V1 coerced int to str. Pydantic V2 rejects int-to-str by default (the `coerce_numbers_to_str` option, per the validator-behavior section, is off by default). At runtime this will raise ValidationError for integer ids. The finding only covers the config class, so I did not add `coerce_numbers_to_str=True` or change the row conversion. Someone should decide whether to add `coerce_numbers_to_str=True` to the ConfigDict (which would preserve V1 behavior) or convert the ids explicitly. Also, `by_assignee` and the other functions are unaffected by the migration."}
